1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Section 1 names four verbal skills: grammar, vocabulary in context, comprehension, sequencing.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
Grammar asks one feature at a time: a sound, a number, a kind of time expression, a fixed particle.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Vocabulary asks for a word where it stands: the sentence, not the dictionary, supplies the meaning.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Comprehension asks what is entailed; sequencing asks what order the sentences must take.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
Agreement is decided by the head of the subject, not by the noun nearest the verb.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
In "the list of items", the head is "list", singular; "of items" is a modifier and changes nothing.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
So the verb is "is". Matching the nearest noun writes "are" — the attraction error.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
"A number of applicants have applied", but "the number of applicants has risen".

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
An article is chosen by the first sound of the next word, not by its first letter.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
"University" begins with the consonant sound /j/, so it takes "a university".

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
"Honorable" begins with a vowel sound because the h is silent, so it takes "an".

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
On the three words of the 2025 item, the letter rule scores one of three; the sound rule scores three.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
Prepositions of time take three kinds of expression: a length, a point, a deadline.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
"For" takes a length, "since" takes a starting point, "within" takes a deadline.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
"For the last three weeks" is right; "since the last three weeks" is not: three weeks is a length.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
The paper's pair is "within six months" and "for the last three weeks".

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
An idiom's particle is part of the word: change it and the meaning changes or vanishes.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
Head down: stay out of trouble. Head out: leave. A head for: a talent. A cool head on your shoulders: composure.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
Four blanks, four fixed particles: down, out, for, on.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Of the paper's four tuples, three break at least two idioms; one breaks none.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
Vocabulary comes in two shapes: a ladder of intensity, and a word defined by its own sentence.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
Dry, arid, parched rises in intensity, so diet, fast, starve must rise the same way.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
"Sacrificed long-term stability to accommodate short-term gains" defines myopic as shortsighted.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Three of the four options point the other way: visionary, progressive, innovative all describe foresight.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
A comprehension question is an entailment question: which option follows from the passage alone?

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
Three premises: most workers are ill, lack of exercise is one leading cause, cycling will be rewarded.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
Only one option follows: the government must believe cycling is exercise.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Three of the four options use an absolute word the passage never uses: all, only, mandatory.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
A jumbled paragraph is a graph: every sentence that points backwards must follow what it points to.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
Mina's four sentences chain three times: bags before panting, stairs before terrace, man before he.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Of the twenty-four orders, exactly one satisfies all three links: (iii), (ii), (i), (iv).

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
That is the fourth option. Each other option breaks at least one of the three links.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
The second shape hides its links in determiners and connectors rather than pronouns.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
"This fortuitous geological event" must follow the collision; "this massive edifice" the risen rocks.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
S, P, R, Q is the only printed option that keeps all three links.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
Two of the four options begin with "Thus", which can only conclude.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
The section reduces to five steps that work on every shape it uses.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
Step one: name what governs the blank — the head noun, the next sound, the time type, the idiom.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Step two: read the passage once without the options, and write its premises in one line each.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
Steps three to five: test each option, strike any addition, and check the answer against the passage.

